WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:19.540
Alexander Fleming: Father of Antibiotics
Scientists are not exactly the most well-known

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:24.010
people in the world, and yet there are a few
names among them which have established themselves

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:26.670
firmly in the public consciousness.

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:32.770
Alexander Fleming is one of those names, inexorably
linked with the discovery of penicillin.

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:37.550
Even people who have never studied science
or medicine in their lives may have still

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:42.540
heard the story of how Fleming’s messiness
accidentally yielded the first antibiotic

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:43.980
in the world.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.940
How he left behind in his lab some Petri dishes
with bacterial cultures and, when he came

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:55.620
back, one of them developed mold which killed
the bacteria and the rest, as they say, is

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:56.620
history.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:58.420
Well, maybe not just yet.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:03.450
As we are about to discover, the real story
was a lot longer and more complicated and

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:06.100
definitely involved a lot more people.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:10.970
History might have preferred to condense it
down to one manageable, bitesize chunk, but

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:15.790
today, however, we are looking at the full
course meal.

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:23.140
Early Years &amp; Education
Alexander Fleming came from humble beginnings.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:29.420
He was born on August 6, 1881, at Lochfield
Farm near the small town of Darvel in Ayrshire,

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:30.420
Scotland.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:33.630
He was the seventh of eight children of Hugh
Fleming, with the last four coming from his

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:35.950
second marriage to Grace Stirling Morton.

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:37.750
Fleming’s father was a farmer.

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:39.540
His mother came from a farming family.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:44.240
He grew up on a large, remote 800-acre farm
that was a mile away from the nearest house.

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:48.640
He and his siblings spent their earliest years
roaming through the nearby valleys, streams,

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:52.690
and moors, ensuring that the future man of
science will always have a fondness and respect

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:53.690
for nature.

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:57.850
The family suffered a major setback in 1888
when Hugh Fleming passed away.

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:02.700
His eldest son from his first marriage took
over the day-to-day operations of the farm.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:06.590
As for Alexander Fleming, or Alec, as the
rest of the family called him, he began his

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:11.760
education at Louden Moor School, a small place
that only had a dozen or so students of different

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.730
ages who were all taught in the same classroom.

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:18.700
From there, he moved on to Darvel School which
was a bit more advanced, but required young

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.910
Alec to walk eight miles each day.

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:26.450
It did not take long for other people to recognize
Fleming’s academic potential and, at age

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:31.890
11, he was awarded a scholarship to Kilmarnock
Academy, a state-funded secondary school and

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:35.950
only one of two schools in the whole United
Kingdom (the other being the prestigious Eton

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:39.200
College) which had educated two Nobel Prize
laureates.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.709
Fleming spent two years at this academy but,
when he was 13, he got the opportunity to

00:02:43.709 --> 00:02:45.420
continue his studies in London.

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:49.980
One of his older brothers, Tom, had moved
to the city to study medicine and later opened

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:50.980
a practice there.

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:54.540
Soon enough, four of his younger siblings,
Alec included, joined him and lived together

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:55.540
in London.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:03:00.310
Fleming enrolled in the Royal Polytechnic
Institution where, again, he impressed with

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:01.650
his book-learning and acumen.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:06.020
He was skipped ahead two years and finished
school when he was just 16.

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:08.910
From there, he needed a job and found work
with a shipping office.

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:14.180
He spent four years at that place, but later
recalled that he did not enjoy the experience.

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:19.640
Going to Med School

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:24.180
In 1900, Alec found a much-needed break from
the work he disliked by joining two of his

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:27.130
brothers in the Second Boer War in South Africa.

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:30.880
All were part of a Volunteer infantry unit
called the London Scottish Regiment.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:35.190
However, they never actually saw any action
before the war ended and mostly spent their

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:37.780
time honing their shooting and swimming skills.

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:41.129
By the time he returned to London, a stroke
of good luck befell Fleming.

00:03:41.129 --> 00:03:46.349
In 1901, when he was 20 years old, he received
an inheritance of roughly 250 pounds from

00:03:46.349 --> 00:03:47.850
his uncle, John Fleming.

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:51.849
It wasn’t a vast fortune or anything, but
it was enough for him to quit his 9-to-5 job

00:03:51.849 --> 00:03:53.019
and continue his studies.

00:03:53.019 --> 00:03:57.780
At the encouragement of his older brother
Tom who now had a thriving medical practice,

00:03:57.780 --> 00:03:58.950
Fleming enrolled in med school.

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:03.209
There is a myth which is still popular today
which states that Fleming’s education was

00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:08.430
paid for by the Churchill family because Fleming’s
father once saved Winston Churchill from drowning

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:09.430
as a boy.

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:13.959
In gratitude, Winston’s father, Lord Randolph
Churchill, promised to give young Alexander

00:04:13.959 --> 00:04:17.009
the same kind of education that his own son
would receive.

00:04:17.009 --> 00:04:21.180
The story continued decades later, during
World War II, when Churchill fell gravely

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:26.729
ill due to pneumonia, but his life was saved,
once again, by a Fleming thanks to the penicillin

00:04:26.729 --> 00:04:28.250
developed by Alexander.

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:33.839
This is quite a fanciful tale and it most
likely still persists today because it involves

00:04:33.839 --> 00:04:37.330
two popular, larger-than-life figures of their
time.

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:41.789
Unfortunately, there is no evidence to back
it up and Fleming himself dismissed it as

00:04:41.789 --> 00:04:43.990
nothing but a “wondrous fable.”

00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:48.120
There is nothing on the Churchill side to
lend it credence, either, and, as you might

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:52.529
suspect, the life of the prime minister has
been extensively documented.

00:04:52.529 --> 00:04:56.699
The simple truth is that Fleming obtained
an inheritance and, because he had excellent

00:04:56.699 --> 00:05:00.219
scores in his examinations, he could, more
or less, have his pick of colleges.

00:05:00.219 --> 00:05:04.069
He chose St. Mary’s Hospital Medical School
in Paddington because it was close to his

00:05:04.069 --> 00:05:07.559
home and because he once played water polo
against their team.

00:05:07.559 --> 00:05:13.090
In 1906, he graduated from the school with
distinction and began his career in medical

00:05:13.090 --> 00:05:14.090
research.

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:18.199
This was not his original choice and, in fact,
he made it through a strange set of circumstances.

00:05:18.199 --> 00:05:20.939
At first, Fleming intended to become a surgeon.

00:05:20.939 --> 00:05:25.949
As it happened, due to his time in the Boer
War, Alec was quite a skilled marksman and

00:05:25.949 --> 00:05:29.169
one of the top shooters on St. Mary’s rifle
club team.

00:05:29.169 --> 00:05:34.039
He was so valued, in fact, that the club captain
did not want him to leave which is what would

00:05:34.039 --> 00:05:35.620
have happened if Fleming became a surgeon.

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:40.830
Instead, he convinced the Scotsman to pursue
a career in research at St. Mary’s and even

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:43.869
got him a position working under Sir Almroth
Wright.

00:05:43.869 --> 00:05:48.169
That way everybody was happy - the rifle club
kept one of its top sharpshooters while Fleming

00:05:48.169 --> 00:05:53.839
got to study under one of the world’s leading
immunologists.

00:05:53.839 --> 00:06:00.069
Early Research
Here, Fleming was exposed to Wright’s ideas

00:06:00.069 --> 00:06:04.009
on vaccine therapies which have, undoubtedly,
influenced his entire career.

00:06:04.009 --> 00:06:09.110
In 1908 he graduated with a Bachelor of Science
degree in Bacteriology and became a lecturer

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:10.169
at St. Mary’s.

00:06:10.169 --> 00:06:14.240
A few years later, Fleming developed a reputation
as the go-to guy in London if you were rich

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:15.240
and had syphilis.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:19.129
He began administering Salvarsan, a new drug
which is known today as arsphenamine.

00:06:19.129 --> 00:06:24.789
It was created in the lab of German physician
and Nobel Prize laureate Paul Ehrlich and,

00:06:24.789 --> 00:06:28.349
back then, it was the first effective treatment
against syphilis.

00:06:28.349 --> 00:06:32.169
Fleming was introduced to the drug by Wright
and amassed a tidy sum of money by being one

00:06:32.169 --> 00:06:34.780
of the main proponents of Salvarsan in England.

00:06:34.780 --> 00:06:39.430
Fleming’s practice and his research progressed
along smoothly until World War I broke out.

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:43.009
He enlisted and served with the Royal Army
Medical Corps as a captain.

00:06:43.009 --> 00:06:47.219
During his time there, he continued his research
into bacteriology in a makeshift laboratory

00:06:47.219 --> 00:06:48.620
in Boulogne, France.

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:53.050
He saw many soldiers die due to infected wounds
and couldn’t help but notice that the antiseptics

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:55.800
they were using tended to make the injuries
worse.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:59.632
He studied this problem and wrote an article
which was subsequently published in the medical

00:06:59.632 --> 00:07:02.069
journal The Lancet in 1917.

00:07:02.069 --> 00:07:07.379
Fleming argued that chemicals such as carbolic
acid which were used as antiseptic were only

00:07:07.379 --> 00:07:10.830
efficient in certain cases such as treating
surface cuts.

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:14.569
When dealing with deeper wounds, the chemicals
did more harm than good as they killed the

00:07:14.569 --> 00:07:20.150
body’s own immunity agents such as leukocytes
and minimized its ability to protect itself

00:07:20.150 --> 00:07:21.279
from infections.

00:07:21.279 --> 00:07:26.719
According to his observations, anaerobic bacteria
thrived in deep wounds despite the presence

00:07:26.719 --> 00:07:27.719
of antiseptics.

00:07:27.719 --> 00:07:32.979
Fleming opined that, in such cases, it was
best to simply keep the wound clean and dry

00:07:32.979 --> 00:07:34.990
and let nature take its course.

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:39.689
His beliefs were echoed by his mentor, Sir
Almroth Wright, who also argued that deep

00:07:39.689 --> 00:07:44.629
wounds should only be cleaned using a simple
saline solution but, unfortunately, their

00:07:44.629 --> 00:08:09.119
recommendations were mostly ignored by field
doctors for the rest of

00:08:09.119 --> 00:08:12.270
the war.

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:53.090
The Discovery of Lysozyme
Even

00:08:53.090 --> 00:08:57.340
though Fleming is renowned for his discovery
of penicillin, he actually made a different

00:08:57.340 --> 00:08:59.190
breakthrough earlier in his career.

00:08:59.190 --> 00:09:03.470
This one, however, did not end up having a
huge impact on medicine so it tends to get

00:09:03.470 --> 00:09:04.470
left behind.

00:09:04.470 --> 00:09:08.680
After he returned home from the war, Fleming
continued his studies into substances with

00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:14.360
microbial properties and made an interesting
discovery while researching nasal mucus.

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:20.060
The exact circumstances are somewhat murky
as different versions of the story were repeated.

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:24.170
Some say he obtained the mucus sample from
a patient with a heavy cold.

00:09:24.170 --> 00:09:28.130
Others say that Fleming himself was working
with a cold and had a “Eureka!” moment

00:09:28.130 --> 00:09:31.100
and decided to investigate his own nasal drippings.

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:35.410
And some say that he simply got lucky and
a bit of his snot dripped down onto a Petri

00:09:35.410 --> 00:09:37.270
dish while he was in the lab.

00:09:37.270 --> 00:09:41.040
Regardless of how it got there, Fleming wanted
to see what effect the mucus would have on

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:42.120
bacteria.

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:44.530
He mixed the two together and let them sit
for a few weeks.

00:09:44.530 --> 00:09:48.300
When he came back to investigate, Fleming
discovered that the nasal substance completely

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:50.630
killed the bacterial colonies.

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:55.610
Fleming realized that the secretion must have
contained an antimicrobial component which

00:09:55.610 --> 00:09:58.700
was part of the human body’s innate immune
system.

00:09:58.700 --> 00:10:02.650
It was an enzyme which Fleming isolated and
named lysozyme.

00:10:02.650 --> 00:10:06.690
As he kept researching this new substance,
he found that it was present in many other

00:10:06.690 --> 00:10:11.000
bodily secretions, including tears, saliva,
skin, and hair.

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:15.720
Subsequently, the bacteriologist discovered
that lysozyme was also present in egg whites

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:20.060
which meant that huge quantities could be
isolated with relative ease.

00:10:20.060 --> 00:10:23.790
This could have been a major boon for the
field of immunology but, unfortunately, the

00:10:23.790 --> 00:10:28.940
enzyme was mostly effective against harmless,
airborne bacteria rather than anything dangerous.

00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:32.620
Today, it is mostly used as a food and wine
preservative.

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:37.380
Even so, it showed Fleming the potential in
this kind of research and set him on track

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:40.231
to make a discovery that will revolutionize
medicine forever.

00:10:40.231 --> 00:10:48.880
Penicillin - A Fortuitous Accident
Fleming once wrote: “When I woke up just

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:54.840
after dawn on September 28, 1928, I certainly
didn’t plan to revolutionize all medicine

00:10:54.840 --> 00:10:58.710
by discovering the world’s first antibiotic,
or bacteria killer.

00:10:58.710 --> 00:11:01.760
But I suppose that was exactly what I did.”

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:04.660
The scientist was looking for a better alternative
to lysozyme.

00:11:04.660 --> 00:11:09.400
The latter showed itself worthy as a proof
of concept, but surely there had to be another

00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:14.180
substance out there somewhere which was more
efficient against dangerous bacteria.

00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:18.200
To that end, Fleming had begun experimenting
and studying the properties of a bacterium

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:23.300
called staphylococcus, a nasty piece of work
that could cause abscesses, sore throats,

00:11:23.300 --> 00:11:24.420
and boils.

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:29.540
Fortunately for Fleming (and the rest of the
world), the bacteriologist was not exactly

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:30.830
a tidy man.

00:11:30.830 --> 00:11:36.970
In August 1928, he went on a family vacation
with his first wife, a nurse named Sarah McElroy,

00:11:36.970 --> 00:11:39.660
and their only child, his 4-year-old boy named
Robert.

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:43.710
He grouped up all of his Petri dishes which
contained bacterial colonies and left them

00:11:43.710 --> 00:11:45.560
near an open window in his lab.

00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:49.500
In some versions of the story, this felicitous
carelessness was ascribed to one of his assistants,

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:51.390
not to Fleming himself.

00:11:51.390 --> 00:11:55.360
When the doctor returned on September 3, he
noticed that one dish had been contaminated

00:11:55.360 --> 00:11:56.560
with mold spores.

00:11:56.560 --> 00:12:00.630
This annoyed him at first, but then he saw
that there were no bacterial colonies near

00:12:00.630 --> 00:12:01.630
the mold.

00:12:01.630 --> 00:12:06.340
The latter had killed all the bacteria it
came into contact with and inhibited its growth.

00:12:06.340 --> 00:12:11.220
Fleming isolated the mold and identified it
as a rare strain of Penicillium notatum.

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:15.440
He gave his new creation the unfortunate name
of “mold juice” and it wasn’t until

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:19.950
several months later that he decided that
a rebranding might be in order and came up

00:12:19.950 --> 00:12:21.200
with penicillin.

00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:24.760
Unappealing name aside, Fleming’s “mold
juice” immediately showed great promise

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:30.750
as it proved effective not only against staphylococcus,
but other harmful bacteria such as meningococcus,

00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:33.570
streptococcus, and the diphtheria bacillus.

00:12:33.570 --> 00:12:38.880
The first antibiotic had been created and
the world now had a potential cure for pneumonia,

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:42.610
gonorrhea, meningitis, scarlet fever, and
other infectious diseases.

00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:45.040
From Mold to Medicine
Actually, not so fast.

00:12:45.040 --> 00:12:50.100
In reality, the process of taking penicillin
and turning it from a simple mold into a practical

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:55.810
medicine was long and difficult and involved
a lot of work from many different scientists.

00:12:55.810 --> 00:13:01.000
Fleming’s role is undeniable, but to give
him sole credit for ushering in the era of

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:04.360
antibiotics is doing a disservice to many
other brilliant minds.

00:13:04.360 --> 00:13:06.650
First off, let’s talk a bit about his precursors.

00:13:06.650 --> 00:13:11.380
Fleming was the first to isolate and identify
the bacteria-killing mold and treat other

00:13:11.380 --> 00:13:15.040
people with it, but the phenomenon itself
had been around for millennia.

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:19.070
Ancient cultures like the Egyptians were noted
for the practice of applying poultices made

00:13:19.070 --> 00:13:21.690
from moldy bread on wounds to prevent infections.

00:13:21.690 --> 00:13:26.600
In modern times, an English physician named
Sir John Burdon-Sanderson might have been

00:13:26.600 --> 00:13:31.940
the first to observe and report that certain
molds from the Penicillium family could inhibit

00:13:31.940 --> 00:13:34.560
the growth of bacteria back in 1871.

00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:39.040
Inspired by his work, Joseph Lister, the father
of antiseptic surgery, established a year

00:13:39.040 --> 00:13:44.370
later that Penicillium glaucum had the potential
to kill off bacteria, but never published

00:13:44.370 --> 00:13:45.420
his findings.

00:13:45.420 --> 00:13:49.210
Many other scientists came before the Scottish
doctor but, for whatever reasons, they were

00:13:49.210 --> 00:13:52.570
never able to take their own research to conclusion.

00:13:52.570 --> 00:13:56.460
Even Fleming himself found it difficult, at
first, to make others care about his discovery.

00:13:56.460 --> 00:14:01.510
In 1928, he isolated the mold, he called it
penicillin and began treating people with

00:14:01.510 --> 00:14:02.510
it.

00:14:02.510 --> 00:14:06.000
In June of the following year, Fleming published
his findings in The British Journal of Experimental

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:09.740
Pathology to a very lukewarm and uninterested
reception.

00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:15.300
Back then, people could not yet see how antibiotics
could change the medical world and this included

00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:16.300
Fleming himself.

00:14:16.300 --> 00:14:20.620
In the article, he made few mentions regarding
penicillin’s potential medical applications

00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:26.480
and, instead, focused on how it could be used
in a mixed culture to separate bacteria sensitive

00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:29.721
to the mold from bacteria that had no reaction
to it.

00:14:29.721 --> 00:14:30.721
The main problem was that Fleming discovered
early on how difficult it was to work with

00:14:30.721 --> 00:14:31.721
penicillin.

00:14:31.721 --> 00:14:33.940
He had two assistants, Stuart Craddock and
Frederick Ridley, who were given the arduous

00:14:33.940 --> 00:14:38.740
task of finding ways to isolate pure penicillin
from the fungus which produced it.

00:14:38.740 --> 00:14:43.440
However, the substance was unstable and they
were only able to produce solutions of crude

00:14:43.440 --> 00:14:44.440
material.

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:48.060
For a while, it looked like manufacturing
large quantities of penicillin would have

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:49.060
been nigh-impossible.

00:14:49.060 --> 00:14:58.100
The Long Road to the Nobel Prize
During the 1930s, Fleming continued his research

00:14:58.100 --> 00:15:02.940
on penicillin, but never made that breakthrough
that would have turned it into the life-saving

00:15:02.940 --> 00:15:04.350
drug we know today.

00:15:04.350 --> 00:15:08.240
The same problems remained all throughout
the decade - the penicillin was too impure

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:10.250
and could not be mass produced.

00:15:10.250 --> 00:15:15.040
That breakthrough did happen, eventually,
but it came from a team at Oxford led by Australian

00:15:15.040 --> 00:15:17.130
pharmacologist Howard Florey.

00:15:17.130 --> 00:15:21.660
His group worked in a research department
named the Sir William Dunn School of Pathology,

00:15:21.660 --> 00:15:23.850
better known simply as the Dunn School.

00:15:23.850 --> 00:15:28.880
They started their work on penicillin in 1939,
with the main goal being to isolate and stabilize

00:15:28.880 --> 00:15:30.790
enough of it to be usable on humans.

00:15:30.790 --> 00:15:35.360
Besides Florey, some of the other key members
of the Oxford team were German biochemist

00:15:35.360 --> 00:15:40.310
Ernst Chain, English biologist Norman Heatley
and English biochemist Edward Abraham.

00:15:40.310 --> 00:15:45.270
However, their program needed so much penicillium
mold filtrate for animal experiments and clinical

00:15:45.270 --> 00:15:49.230
trials that, soon enough, pretty much the
entire Dunn School worked on the project so

00:15:49.230 --> 00:15:54.190
we could never fully give credit to everyone
who played a part in developing the life-saving

00:15:54.190 --> 00:15:55.190
antibiotic.

00:15:55.190 --> 00:15:59.140
Their efforts were also slowed down by another
“minor issue” called World War II so it

00:15:59.140 --> 00:16:02.900
wasn’t like they had an abundance of manpower
and resources.

00:16:02.900 --> 00:16:07.540
In fact, to save on space and materials, they
began growing bacteria cultures in whatever

00:16:07.540 --> 00:16:12.600
vessels they had on hand such as bathtubs,
food tins, and even bedpans.

00:16:12.600 --> 00:16:16.800
Edward Abraham correctly deduced the structure
of penicillin, at roughly the same time as

00:16:16.800 --> 00:16:18.880
American chemist Robert Burns Woodward.

00:16:18.880 --> 00:16:23.260
Norman Heatley suggested that larger volumes
of penicillin could be produced by extracting

00:16:23.260 --> 00:16:27.120
it into amyl acetate and then back into water.

00:16:27.120 --> 00:16:31.010
Chain and Florey discovered how to isolate
and concentrate the germ-killing agent found

00:16:31.010 --> 00:16:32.020
in penicillin.

00:16:32.020 --> 00:16:36.060
In 1940, the team used penicillin to protect
mice from streptococcus.

00:16:36.060 --> 00:16:40.000
A year later, a policeman named Albert Alexander
became the first human to receive the Oxford

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:43.779
penicillin in order to treat a bad infection
he had inside his mouth.

00:16:43.779 --> 00:16:47.160
Although Alexander made a quick recovery in
just a few days, they didn’t have enough

00:16:47.160 --> 00:16:52.250
of the drug to deal with the infection completely
and it returned in a few days and killed him.

00:16:52.250 --> 00:16:57.060
Not an auspicious start, but subsequent patients
fared better and showed the world the value

00:16:57.060 --> 00:16:59.120
of penicillin as medicine.

00:16:59.120 --> 00:17:02.890
The challenge of scaling up production of
the drug to industrial quantities was eventually

00:17:02.890 --> 00:17:07.480
taken up by British and American pharmaceutical
companies, particularly the Northern Regional

00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:12.579
Research Laboratory (NRRL) in Peoria, Illinois,
a subsidiary of the United States Department

00:17:12.579 --> 00:17:13.579
of Agriculture.

00:17:13.579 --> 00:17:17.789
As you can see, there were many individuals
and institutions that played a vital role

00:17:17.789 --> 00:17:20.650
in the development of penicillin as an antibiotic.

00:17:20.650 --> 00:17:25.420
For their work, they were awarded the Nobel
Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 1945, although

00:17:25.420 --> 00:17:30.190
it only recognized Alexander Fleming, Howard
Florey, and Ernst Chain due to one of the

00:17:30.190 --> 00:17:35.570
foundation’s most controversial rules of
awarding a Nobel Prize to a maximum of three

00:17:35.570 --> 00:17:37.960
people.

00:17:37.960 --> 00:17:44.679
Later Years
Fleming spent his entire professional career

00:17:44.679 --> 00:17:50.470
as a researcher at St. Mary’s and, in 1946,
succeeded his mentor Almroth Wright as head

00:17:50.470 --> 00:17:55.419
of the hospital’s Inoculation Department
which was renamed to the Wright-Fleming Institute.

00:17:55.419 --> 00:18:00.000
He also served as President of the Society
for General Microbiology, rector of Edinburgh

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:04.379
University, a member of the Pontifical Academy
of Science, and even as Honorary Chief Doy-gei-tau

00:18:04.379 --> 00:18:05.710
of the Native American Kiowa tribe.

00:18:05.710 --> 00:18:08.529
In addition to these, Fleming also received
pretty much every honorary medal and doctorate

00:18:08.529 --> 00:18:10.540
and award and all other scientific honors
under the sun.

00:18:10.540 --> 00:18:14.260
For his part, Fleming always made sure to
acknowledge the efforts of Florey and his

00:18:14.260 --> 00:18:19.110
team in making penicillin a worldwide commodity,
but the general public and the media seemed

00:18:19.110 --> 00:18:24.419
determined to lavish their praise solely on
the Scottish microbiologist, probably because

00:18:24.419 --> 00:18:27.950
the story of his “happy accident” was
more enjoyable.

00:18:27.950 --> 00:18:31.669
In his later years, Fleming concentrated his
efforts on warning people about a problem

00:18:31.669 --> 00:18:35.379
that we face right now - resistance to antibiotics.

00:18:35.379 --> 00:18:39.309
Both he and Almroth Wright realized early
on that microbes could very easily become

00:18:39.309 --> 00:18:44.019
resistant to penicillin if the wrong doses
were used or for too short a period, with

00:18:44.019 --> 00:18:48.830
each subsequent generation of microorganisms
being more resistant than the last.

00:18:48.830 --> 00:18:52.419
He wrote several papers on the matter and
even mentioned it during his speech for the

00:18:52.419 --> 00:18:53.429
Nobel Prize.

00:18:53.429 --> 00:18:56.039
In his private life, Fleming was quiet and
unassuming.

00:18:56.039 --> 00:19:00.179
His son Robert followed in his father’s
footsteps, studied medicine and became a general

00:19:00.179 --> 00:19:01.179
practitioner.

00:19:01.179 --> 00:19:05.919
His first wife, Sarah, passed away in 1949
and, unsurprisingly, this had a profound effect

00:19:05.919 --> 00:19:10.740
on Fleming who buried himself in his work
and spent a lot of time alone, in his laboratory,

00:19:10.740 --> 00:19:12.360
until the late hours of the night.

00:19:12.360 --> 00:19:16.450
Fleming’s life brightened up considerably
when he met Dr. Amalia Koutsouri-Vourekas

00:19:16.450 --> 00:19:18.460
who came to work at St. Mary’s.

00:19:18.460 --> 00:19:22.659
The two fell in love and married in 1953,
but their happiness was short-lived.

00:19:22.659 --> 00:19:28.510
Alexander Fleming died suddenly two years
later, on March 11, 1955, of coronary thrombosis.

00:19:28.510 --> 00:19:32.110
He had been feeling poorly over the last few
weeks, but was convinced that it was only

00:19:32.110 --> 00:19:33.419
gastric distress.

00:19:33.419 --> 00:19:37.149
When he started feeling nauseous on the day
of his death, his wife wanted to call a doctor,

00:19:37.149 --> 00:19:39.749
but he insisted that it wasn’t necessary.

00:19:39.749 --> 00:19:43.690
He had a heart attack minutes later and was
subsequently buried at St Paul’s Cathedral.

00:19:43.690 --> 00:19:49.470
Alexander Fleming’s life may have been extinguished
in moments, but he left behind a life-saving

00:19:49.470 --> 00:20:00.679
legacy that will live on forever, one that
few people in history could ever hope

00:20:00.679 --> 00:20:09.900
to match.

